Handling Millions of Requests

During one of my interviews, the interviewer asked me a classic scalability question:

“Imagine we’re getting millions of requests per minutes, and all we need to do is insert them into the database. How would you handle that efficiently?”

Problem :

If every incoming request directly writes to the DB, we’ll quickly hit limits:
 • Too many open DB connections
 • Lock contention
 • I/O bottlenecks
 • Reduced throughput

So the first goal is to decouple ingestion from persistence.

Solution : Kafka + Batch Inserts

1. Ingestion Layer:
Every incoming request first goes into Apache Kafka — a high-throughput, distributed message broker.
 • Kafka acts as a buffer between the incoming traffic and the database.
 • It ensures we never lose data, even if the DB slows down.

2. Consumer Layer:
We have a Kafka Consumer Service running in multiple instances for scalability.
 • Each consumer reads messages in batches (say 500–1000 records).
 • Instead of inserting each record individually, we use batch inserts or bulk writes into the database.

This approach drastically reduces round trips and improves write throughput.

3. Database Optimization:
 • Use connection pooling
 • Consider partitioned tables for scalability
 • If writes are still heavy, move to a distributed database like Cassandra or ClickHouse

The Result:
• Smooth handling of millions of incoming requests
• No data loss thanks to Kafka durability
• Massive reduction in DB load due to batch inserts
• Easy horizontal scalability

In high-scale systems, you don’t scale by working harder — you scale by decoupling.
Kafka gives you the breathing room.
Batch processing gives you the performance.

